<main class="sheet claims-page">
  <p class="headline">Everything a person can do in a Cairn app is a claim: an id, a promise, and a proof that the promise holds.</p>
  <p class="lede">Not a test that passed once, a statement Lean checks for every state the app can be
    in. Here is one running.</p>

  {{{ $utils.replay() }}}

  <p>Each step of that run is a move with its own id, and each move promises what follows, in
    words for people and in a statement for Lean. The flow above, <code>^216</code>, is itself a
    claim: Lean checks that every step is offered when it's taken and that the goal holds at the
    end. Change the app so that any of it stops being true, and it doesn't build.</p>

  <h2 id="anatomy">What's in one</h2>
  <p>Here is the whole of Reset, in Counter. The top half is for people; everything under it is
    for the checker.</p>
  {{{ $utils.anatomy() }}}

  <h2 id="what-they-buy">What they buy you</h2>

  <h3 class="sub" id="every-state">Every state, not one run</h3>
  <p>A test drives the app down one path and checks where it lands. A claim's proof is about
    every state at once, including the ones nobody thought to try.</p>
  {{{ $utils.stateField() }}}

  <h3 class="sub" id="nothing-unclaimed">Nothing happens without one</h3>
  <div class="gate">
    <div class="g-in px"><p class="gh">An event arrives</p><p>a click, a key, a timer</p></div>
    <div class="g-split">
      <div class="g-yes px"><p class="gh"><span class="id">^N</span> owns it</p><p>Its move runs, and its promise holds afterwards.</p></div>
      <div class="g-no px"><p class="gh">No claim owns it</p><p>The state is unchanged and nothing is asked of the host. <code>update_unclaimed</code></p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>An app's update is built only from its moves, so this holds for every Cairn app, proved
    once in the framework. Every effect an app asks for (a file write, a sound, a timer) comes
    from some move (<code>update_effect</code>). The list of claims is the list of what the app
    can do, with nothing hiding outside it.</p>

  <h3 class="sub" id="one-name">One name, everywhere</h3>
  <p>The id is how everything else refers to the behaviour, and it means the same thing in all
    of them:</p>
  <ul class="names">
    <li><span class="where">The code</span><code>move ^204 toggleTodo (s : State) (k : Nat) "Complete a todo"</code></li>
    <li><span class="where">Accessibility</span><code>identifier "^204#1"</code><i>the checkbox on milk, for VoiceOver and UI tests</i></li>
    <li><span class="where">A flow</span><code>witness … submit ^202, press ^204#1</code></li>
    <li><span class="where">Its page</span><code>/packages/cairn_examples/claims/204/</code></li>
    <li><span class="where">Review</span><code>^204 “Complete a todo”: meaning changed</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p>A <code>data-testid</code> names an element and drifts when someone renames it. <code>^204</code>
    names a behaviour, and can't be renamed: tests, docs, agents and bug reports that cite it stay
    right for as long as the app exists.</p>

  <h3 class="sub" id="ledger">Meaning changes show up in review</h3>
  <p>Each package keeps a ledger of every id it has issued. A refactor that changes no promise
    passes quietly; one that changes what a claim says is flagged, and has to take a new id or be
    accepted in a commit someone reviews.</p>
  {{{ $utils.ledger() }}}
  <p>A released claim is a promise to whoever relies on it, so retiring one needs a major
    version: semver for guarantees, checked by the build.</p>

  <h2 id="kinds">Six kinds</h2>
  <p>Moves are what people do. The other five say how moves fit together, what always holds, and
    what is taken on trust.</p>
  {{{ $utils.kinds() }}}

  <h2 id="how-sure">How sure we are</h2>
  <p>Each claim carries the evidence it has, not only the strongest. They rule out different
    things:</p>
  <ol class="rungs">
    <li class="r1"><b>Proved</b><span>A counterexample anywhere: every state, every key, every input.</span></li>
    <li class="r2"><b>Fuzzed</b><span>A counterexample in the random sessions it ran, the claim checked after every step.</span></li>
    <li class="r3"><b>Tested</b><span>The running app disagreeing with the proved model, on the sequences it drove.</span></li>
    <li class="r4"><b>Seen</b><span>A control that's never drawn, a flow that can't be clicked through.</span></li>
    <li class="r5"><b>Assumed</b><span>Nothing by itself. Written down, so every proof that relies on it says so.</span></li>
  </ol>
  <p>A proof still trusts that its statement says what you mean: <code>post s' := True</code> is
    easy to prove and promises nothing. <a href="/docs/reference/trust/">How much to trust a
    claim</a> says what each kind of evidence still takes on faith, with a bug the tests miss and
    the proof catches.</p>

